integrated_rules:
Let P be a proposed expansion of socialist-style policy in the U.S. Define:

AG1_support (supports becoming more socialist) iff all of the following are satisfied: 
1) Socialist-style programs have already proven successful in the U.S.;
2) Further socialist measures are justified as a response to extreme inequality and the harms of unfettered capitalism—i.e., the top 1% holds more wealth than the bottom 50% and socialist measures are expected to mitigate those harms even while operating alongside capitalist structures;
3) Core socialist policies have broad U.S. public support, specifically including polling support at about these levels: 69% back Medicare for all, 63% support free college tuition, 85% support paid work leave, and 64% support a wealth tax that can finance redistribution.

AG2_opposition (supports not becoming more socialist) iff all of the following are satisfied:
1) Upward mobility would be expected to decrease because the policy reduces work incentives; 
2) There is no available evidence (in the relevant case) that P preserves work incentives for low- and moderate-income earners; 
3) The proposed socialist package is estimated to cost about $42.5 trillion over ten years; 
4) The political-ethical premise is adopted that the U.S. government’s job is to promote equal opportunity, not equal results; 
5) Empirical-predictive expectations are adopted that countries prioritizing economic freedom achieve higher life expectancy, political and civil liberties, gender equality, and happiness; 
6) Polling indicates U.S. support for capitalism, specifically about: 57% favor capitalism, 84% support free enterprise, and 60% believe free markets work best.

Integrated decision rule:
- If AG1_support(P) is true and AG2_opposition(P) is false → conclude: “the United States should become more socialist.”
- If AG2_opposition(P) is true and AG1_support(P) is false → conclude: “the United States should not become more socialist.”
- If both AG1_support(P) and AG2_opposition(P) are true, or if neither is true → leave the conclusion unresolved pending additional evidence.
